<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Indian Civil Service</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Indian Civil Service was the elite administrative service of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, recruited by competitive examination. A small number of officials governed districts and provinces across the subcontinent. It was a central institution of British imperial governance and continued in altered form after independence.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Officially the Imperial Civil Service, the ICS was the higher civil service of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">British Raj</a> between 1858 and 1947. Until 1853 the directors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> appointed covenanted civil servants by nomination; that year Parliament abolished the system and decided that appointments would be made by competitive examination of British subjects, without distinction of race. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-rebellion-of-1857">Indian Rebellion of 1857</a> ended the Company's rule, the service took over its administrative work, its members appointed under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/government-of-india-act-1858">Government of India Act 1858</a> and headed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secretary-of-state-for-india">Secretary of State for India</a>, a member of the British Cabinet.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The examination was held at first only in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, and candidates aged between 18 and 23 had to pass a horse-riding test. Successful candidates spent one or two years of probation at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, colleges of the University of London or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinity-college-dublin">Trinity College Dublin</a>, studying Indian law, history and the language of their province.</p><p>Little more than a thousand Civilians oversaw government in 250 districts and more than 300 million people. Assistant commissioners began on around £300 a year, provincial governors earned £6,000, and every officer retired on a pension of £1,000. A dress uniform was allowed only to officers whose political duties brought them into frequent contact with Indian princes. No women were ever recruited to the service.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> 95% of the officers were Europeans. As British recruits grew scarce, Indians filled 44% of new appointments between 1915 and 1924, and from 1922 Indians could sit the examination in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>. In 1924 the Lee Commission, chaired by Arthur Lee, 1st Viscount Lee of Fareham, recommended that future intakes be 40% European, 40% Indian and 20% promoted from the provincial services. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> examinations were suspended after 1939, and the last officers joined in 1943.</p><p>In 1947 there were 980 officers, 468 of them British. The service was divided between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, where it became the Indian Administrative Service, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, where it became the Civil Service of Pakistan. Jawaharlal Nehru had often ridiculed it, quoting a definition of it as neither Indian, nor civil, nor a service, but as Prime Minister he kept its people, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vallabhbhai-patel">Sardar Patel</a> told Parliament that without them the country would have collapsed. V.M.M. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nair">Nair</a>, the last surviving former officer, died in 2021.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Indian Civil Service” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Indian Civil Service</b> ran a country of some 300 million people with a cadre of only about 1,000 to 1,200 senior officers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>…the Punjab and the United Provinces, were run by governors and lieutenant-governors, with districts under collectors and magistrates drawn from the <b>Indian Civil Service</b>, an elite of about a thousand officials recruited by competitive examination, which Indians could enter but which remained mostly British until the late 1930s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>About three-fifths of the subcontinent formed the provinces of British India, such as Bengal, Bombay and Madras, governed by a viceroy and a small corps of British officials, the <b>Indian Civil Service</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>The viceroy governed in the Crown’s name, and a little over a thousand men of the <b>Indian Civil Service</b>, almost all of them British, ran the districts; Indians held the lower posts.</blockquote><a href="/societies/british-raj">Social Hierarchy of British India</a></li></ul></section>
